```

In this code, the given JSON string is converted into a JSON object using the JSON.parse() function. Then it is printed with proper indentation using JSON.stringify(). Then it is validated against the defined schema. The Ajv library allows us to define a schema and validate JSON data against it very easily. If the JSON structure does not match the schema, the validate function will return false and print the errors.

Here is your JavaScript code:

```javascript
// The function lowest_common_multiple takes in two parameters, x and y
// The parameters x and y are expected to be lists of integers
// The function calculates the lowest common multiple for each pair of integers in the input arrays
function lowest_common_multiple(x, y) {
    // Map over the array by index
    return x.map((num, idx) => {
        // Use the built-in algorithm to calculate the gcd
        const gcd = (a, b) => b === 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b);

        // computedGcd stores the greatest common divisor of the pair of numbers
        const computedGcd = gcd(num, y[idx]);

        // absProduct is the absolute product of the pair of numbers
        const absProduct = Math.abs(num*y[idx]);

        // finally, we divide absProduct by computedGcd to get the lcm
        return absProduct / computedGcd ;
    });
}

// Test 
console.log(lowest_common_multiple([3, 5], [5, 15])); // [15, 75]
```
This JavaScript code works in a similar way to your Python code. The `map` function is similar to the list comprehension in Python, and applies a function to each element in the array. In this case the provided anonymous function takes the LCM of each pair of elements from the two arrays.

