```java
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class QuadExpression {

    private AtomicInteger result;
    private int k, n;

    public QuadExpression(int k, int n) {
        if(n < k) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid interval, k should be <= n");
        }

        result = new AtomicInteger(0);
        this.k = k;
        this.n = n;
    }

    public int execute() {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

        for (int i = k; i <= n; i++) {
            final int num = i;
            if(i % 2 == 0) {
                executor.execute(new Runnable() {
                    public void run() {
                        int quadExp = num * num;
                        result.getAndAdd(quadExp);
                    }
                });
            }
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        try {
            // Waiting for all threads to finish
            executor.awaitTermination(Long.MAX_VALUE, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return result.get();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QuadExpression quadExp = new QuadExpression(2, 10);
        System.out.println("The cumulative total of quadratic expressions: " + quadExp.execute());

        try {
            QuadExpression ex = new QuadExpression(10, 2);
        } catch(IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
In this solved program, we are making use of ExecutorService to execute multiple threads to concurrently calculate the quadratic expressions for even numbers. AtomicInteger is used to handle concurrent updates on the cumulative total. 

Please note that this program does not check if n > k which may cause the program to run indefinitely if (n - k + 1) threads are more than the thread limit of your system. The 'if(n < k)' part in the code is only for checking if the end of the range is smaller than starting of the range. If 'n' is not greater or equal to 'k', an exception is thrown.

Do not forget to properly handle the InterruptedException because the main thread is waiting for all the spawned threads to finish.

